<edge source="document" target="has">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation "document, has" encompasses a variety of attributes and elements associated with a document. A document within this context contains a manifest JSON file for validation purposes, ensuring the integrity of the document's structure. Additionally, the document checks for missing or mismatched image sizes, which is crucial for maintaining consistency in visual content. To further guarantee data integrity, the document includes a SHA256 hash, which serves as a means for integrity checks.

The document also manages the force reparsing of DOCLING by setting a flag, which suggests a level of configurability in how the document is processed. It is designed to contain images for processing, which indicates its role in handling multimedia content. Furthermore, a document in this relation is equipped with text unit IDs for the parsed text chunks, facilitating the organization and manipulation of textual data.

In terms of metadata, a document has the capability to store metadata if specified during the import process, enhancing its descriptive power. The document's title is typically derived from the filename, which is a common practice for easy identification and organization. Lastly, the document contains the full text content, which is the core component of the document and is essential for its primary function.

Overall, the "document, has" relation is characterized by its comprehensive nature, encompassing various elements such as validation, image size checks, integrity hashes, configurability, metadata, titles, and full text content, all of which contribute to the document's functionality and usability.</data>
  <data key="d9">_manifest.json,`LIGHTRAG_FORCE_REPARSE_DOCLING=true,full text,image,image missing or size mismatch,metadata,sha256,text unit IDs,title</data>
